# =============================================================================
# Doctor Visit Tracker — environment variables
#
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE (for a non-technical person)
# -------------------------------------------------
# 1. Make a copy of this file in the same folder and name the copy exactly:
#        .env
# 2. Fill in the values by copying them from your Supabase project page.
#    Instructions with screenshots: docs/90-setup-for-non-technical.md
# 3. NEVER send the .env file to anyone, and never put it on GitHub.
#    The .gitignore file already blocks it.
#
# This .env.example file contains NO real secrets and is safe to share.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase connection (REQUIRED)
#
# Find both values in Supabase:  Project Settings -> API
#
# The "anon" key is designed to be inside a mobile app. It is safe there ONLY
# because every table has Row Level Security switched on. Do not disable RLS.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=paste-your-anon-public-key-here

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEVER put the service_role key in this file or anywhere in the app.
#
# The service_role key ignores all security rules. If it ends up inside the
# mobile app, anyone who downloads the app can read and change everything.
# It belongs only on a server or in your CI system's secret store.
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_ anything is compiled INTO the app and readable by anyone.
# That prefix must only ever be used for values that are safe in public.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local database, for developers running the automated tests (OPTIONAL)
#
# These are standard PostgreSQL settings. Leave them alone unless a developer
# tells you otherwise. They are NOT used by the phone app.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
PGHOST=localhost
PGPORT=5432
PGUSER=postgres
PGPASSWORD=postgres
TEST_DATABASE=dvt_test

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Power BI reporting login (OPTIONAL — server side only, never in the app)
#
# Create this read-only database user by following docs/70-powerbi-guide.md.
# Store the password in Power BI's own credential box, not in this file.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REPORTING_DB_HOST=db.your-project-ref.supabase.co
# REPORTING_DB_USER=reporting_reader
